Save the configuration when the options screen is left.

Upstream calls `config_save` from exactly one place - `putt/main.c:386`, after the main
loop returns. On a desktop that is sound: quitting is how a game ends, so every setting
is written on the way out.

A console title does not end that way. It is closed from the dashboard or killed, `main`
never returns, and every volume and graphics choice the player made in the options screen
is lost with it.

Leaving the options screen is the narrowest place that catches all of them at once, and -
unlike saving from a close handler - it does not have to be right about how much time the
system's kill timer allows. Neverball carries the same change in the same place; it also
saves when the player's name is entered, which Neverputt has no screen for, so this is the
whole of it here.

This is the title's first patch. It stayed at none deliberately until something was
opaque or broken, and settings evaporating every launch is the latter.

diff --git a/putt/st_conf.c b/putt/st_conf.c
index fa01b57..e7ca09d 100644
--- a/putt/st_conf.c
+++ b/putt/st_conf.c
@@ -49,6 +49,16 @@ static int conf_action(int i)
     switch (i)
     {
     case CONF_BACK:
+        /* **The settings are written here, not only at exit.** Upstream calls `config_save`
+         * from one place - `putt/main.c:386`, after the main loop returns - which is right on a
+         * desktop, where quitting is how a game ends. A console title is closed from the
+         * dashboard or killed, so `main` never returns and every choice made in this screen is
+         * lost with it.
+         *
+         * Neverball fixes the same thing in the same place. It also saves when the player's
+         * name is entered, which Neverputt has no screen for - so this is the whole of it
+         * here. */
+        config_save();
         goto_state(&st_title);
         break;
 
